# Copy to .env and fill in real values (.env is gitignored).
# The API connects as the RLS-constrained application role (mosaiz_app_user),
# NEVER as postgres — RLS isolation depends on it. Local DB is PG18 on 5433.
# In production, size the pool for the deployment and cap the wait, e.g.:
#   ...mosaiz_mundo?connection_limit=20&pool_timeout=10
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://mosaiz_app_user:PASSWORD@localhost:5433/mosaiz_mundo"
PORT=3000

# Modern Supabase projects sign access tokens with an ES256 key. This is that
# key's PUBLIC half (PEM, with \n escapes), derived from the project JWKS at
# <project>/auth/v1/.well-known/jwks.json. The auth middleware verifies ES256
# tokens with this and HS256 tokens with SUPABASE_JWT_SECRET below.
SUPABASE_JWT_PUBLIC_KEY="-----BEGIN PUBLIC KEY-----\n...\n-----END PUBLIC KEY-----"

# Comma-separated browser origins allowed by CORS (the admin app). Requests
# with no Origin (Android POS, curl, webhooks) are always allowed.
CORS_ORIGINS="http://localhost:5173"

# Max requests per IP per minute (per-IP flood backstop). Default 600.
RATE_LIMIT_MAX=600

# Set to 1 (or the hop count) ONLY when behind a trusted reverse proxy, so the
# real client IP is used for rate limiting. Leave unset for direct deployments.
# TRUST_PROXY=1

# Supabase Auth (external IdP) — the project's JWT secret (HS256). The API
# verifies every bearer token against this; the token's `sub` claim is the
# user UUID bound to app.current_user_id for RLS. Get it from the Supabase
# dashboard: Project Settings -> API -> JWT Settings.
SUPABASE_JWT_SECRET="your-supabase-jwt-secret"

# Shared secret used to verify the HMAC-SHA256 signature on the Supabase
# identity webhook (POST /api/webhooks/supabase). Set the same value in the
# Supabase Database Webhook / Edge Function that signs the payload.
SUPABASE_WEBHOOK_SECRET="your-supabase-webhook-secret"

# --- Logo upload (0032 / Supabase Storage) -----------------------------------
# The project URL, and the SERVICE ROLE key from Project Settings -> API.
#
# The service role bypasses Row Level Security, so it stays on the server and is
# never sent to a browser. It is needed because a Supabase Storage policy cannot
# see this project's organization_memberships table — those live in our own
# Postgres — so it could only ever have allowed "any signed-in user", which
# includes every cashier. The role check happens where the roles are.
#
# Without these, POST /api/branding/logo answers 503 naming the missing one;
# nothing else is affected.
SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project.supabase.co
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cache (optional). Leave UNSET and the API serves everything from Postgres —
# that is a supported configuration, not a degraded one, and it is what CI runs.
#
# Only two reads are cached, both hot and neither money-shaped: the till's menu
# and the review rubric. Reports, exports and anything with a margin in it are
# deliberately never cached, because a stale figure there changes a decision.
#
# Every key carries the organization AND the role, because the same url returns
# different data depending on who asked.
#
# Absent means NO CACHE, and that is a supported state: every read goes to
# Postgres. Nothing breaks, it is only slower.
#
# On Windows, run redis in DOCKER, not in WSL. That is the conclusion of
# actually chasing this down, and the WSL route is recorded here only so nobody
# repeats it:
#
#   * redis inside WSL binds to 127.0.0.1 in the WSL network namespace, which a
#     Windows-hosted API cannot reach.
#   * networkingMode=mirrored in %USERPROFILE%\.wslconfig sounds like the fix
#     and is not — it BREAKS loopback forwarding, so it trades one unreachable
#     address for another. NAT + localhostForwarding does work.
#   * the Hyper-V firewall governing WSL traffic defaults to
#     DefaultInboundAction=Block, so it also needs one rule (admin PowerShell):
#
#     New-NetFirewallHyperVRule -Name MosaizRedis6379 `
#       -DisplayName 'Redis 6379 (WSL)' -Direction Inbound `
#       -VMCreatorId '{40E0AC32-46A5-438A-A0B2-2B479E8F2E90}' `
#       -Protocol TCP -LocalPorts 6379 -Action Allow
#
#   * and even then the WSL VM idles out and takes redis with it. vmIdleTimeout
#     was removed in WSL 2.6.3, so there is no longer a knob for it.
#
# Docker publishes the port on the Windows host directly and sidesteps all of
# it:  docker run -d --name mosaiz-redis -p 127.0.0.1:6379:6379 redis:7-alpine
#
# Do NOT bind redis to 0.0.0.0 instead: that puts it on every network this
# machine joins, unauthenticated.
# REDIS_URL=redis://127.0.0.1:6379

# Log verbosity: debug | info | warn | error. Defaults to info. An unrecognised
# value falls back to info (and is warned about at boot) rather than silencing
# the log or, worse, passing everything through.
#
# Output is JSON Lines on stdout in production, and a readable line otherwise.
# Nothing is logged unless it is named in src/lib/logger.ts — no headers, no
# bodies, no query strings.
# LOG_LEVEL=info

# Optional ceiling on a JSON request body. Defaults to 256kb.
# JSON_BODY_LIMIT=256kb

# The pool is the concurrency ceiling: one request holds one connection for its
# whole life (that is how the RLS identity is bound). The API appends
# connection_limit=20&pool_timeout=10 to DATABASE_URL when they are absent, so
# set them explicitly here to choose your own.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PRODUCTION (NODE_ENV=production)
#
# The API checks its configuration once at boot and REFUSES to start on a
# fatal one, rather than starting and answering 500 on every request while
# /health cheerfully reports ok. See src/config.ts.
#
# Required in production, on top of the above:
#   CORS_ORIGINS   — the real admin origin. Unset, only the localhost default
#                    is allowed and the browser blocks the admin app with
#                    nothing at all in the server logs.
#
# Warned about, because they are usually wrong but not always:
#   TRUST_PROXY    — set to the hop count behind a load balancer, or every
#                    request looks like it came from the proxy and all users
#                    share one per-IP rate limit.
#   REDIS_URL      — absent is supported, just slower.
#
# Refused outright: a DATABASE_URL connecting as postgres. A superuser bypasses
# RLS, which would disable every tenant boundary in the system.
#
# Two probes, and they are not interchangeable:
#   /health  liveness  — process is up. No database. A bad answer means RESTART.
#   /ready   readiness — database reachable. A bad answer means STOP ROUTING.
